GUIDES = [
dict(slug="how-to-apply-for-a-loan-step-by-step", title="How to Apply for a Loan in India: A Step-by-Step Guide", cat="Basics",
 desc="A clear, step-by-step guide to applying for any loan in India, from checking your credit report to signing the agreement and receiving funds.",
 body="""<p>Applying for a loan is easier when you follow a fixed order. This process works for almost every loan type, from a small personal loan to a large home loan.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Define the need and the amount</h2><p>Write down what you need the money for and the exact amount, including fees. Borrowing more than needed raises your interest cost for no benefit.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Check your credit report</h2><p>Get your credit report from a credit bureau. Look for errors, missed payments and high card utilisation. Fix what you can before you apply.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Work out what you can repay</h2><p>As a rule of thumb, keep all EMIs, including the new one, within 40% to 50% of your net monthly income. Use our <a href="/tools/emi-calculator/">EMI calculator</a> to test different amounts and tenures.</p>
<h2>Step 4: Shortlist lenders</h2><p>Compare at least three offers on APR, processing fees, prepayment terms and total repayment. Banks, NBFCs and digital lenders each have different strengths. NEXUS can help you compare through <a href="/apply/">one simple form</a>.</p>
<h2>Step 5: Keep documents ready</h2><p>KYC, income proof, bank statements and any asset papers. Use our <a href="/tools/document-checklist/">document checklist tool</a> to see the exact list for your loan type.</p>
<h2>Step 6: Apply and complete KYC</h2><p>Apply on the lender's own secure platform or at the branch. Verification may include video KYC, employer or business checks and, for secured loans, property or asset valuation.</p>
<h2>Step 7: Read the sanction letter and Key Fact Statement</h2><p>Check the APR, EMI, tenure, all charges, prepayment terms and the grievance process before accepting. Never sign a blank form.</p>
<h2>Step 8: Disbursal and repayment</h2><p>Funds should be credited directly to your bank account. Set up auto-debit and keep a small buffer in the account to avoid bounced EMIs.</p>
<p><strong>Remember:</strong> never pay an upfront fee to anyone to 'guarantee' loan approval. Legitimate fees are charged by the lender and shown in the Key Fact Statement.</p>"""),
dict(slug="how-to-improve-cibil-score", title="How to Improve Your CIBIL and Credit Score in India", cat="Credit score",
 desc="Practical, proven ways to raise your credit score: pay on time, lower utilisation, fix errors, and avoid too many enquiries.",
 body="""<p>Your credit score summarises your repayment behaviour. In India, scores from the credit bureaus usually range from 300 to 900. Many lenders prefer 700 or above, and the best rates often go to those with 750+.</p>
<h2>What affects your score most</h2><ul><li><strong>Payment history:</strong> late or missed EMIs and card dues are the biggest negatives.</li><li><strong>Credit utilisation:</strong> using more than about 30% to 40% of your card limits can pull your score down.</li><li><strong>Credit mix:</strong> a healthy mix of secured and unsecured credit, managed well, helps.</li><li><strong>Enquiries:</strong> many loan applications in a short time can reduce your score temporarily.</li><li><strong>Credit age:</strong> older accounts in good standing help.</li></ul>
<h2>Seven steps to improve it</h2><ol><li>Set auto-debit for EMIs and card bills, so you never miss a due date.</li><li>Pay card bills in full where possible.</li><li>Keep card utilisation low by paying early or raising your limit.</li><li>Do not close your oldest card without a reason.</li><li>Dispute errors with the bureau and the lender, and follow up in writing.</li><li>Apply for credit only when needed.</li><li>Settle, do not ignore, overdue accounts. A 'settled' status is better than default but still shows on your report, so aim to repay in full.</li></ol>
<h2>How long does it take?</h2><p>Small improvements can appear in 1 to 3 months after corrections. Rebuilding after serious defaults can take a year or more. Credit information companies update data periodically, so changes are not instant.</p>
<h2>Check your own report</h2><p>You are entitled to a free full credit report from each credit bureau at least once a year, and recent RBI rules have widened free access. Check each bureau's current policy. Checking your own score is a 'soft enquiry' and does not hurt it.</p>
<p>Not sure where you stand? <a href="/contact/">Talk to us</a>. We can suggest the loan types you may qualify for today.</p>"""),
dict(slug="what-is-emi-and-how-is-it-calculated", title="What Is an EMI and How Is It Calculated?", cat="Basics",
 desc="Understand EMI, the formula behind it, how interest and principal split over time, and how tenure and rate change your monthly payment.",
 body="""<p>EMI stands for Equated Monthly Instalment: the fixed amount you pay every month until the loan is fully repaid. Each EMI contains two parts, interest and principal.</p>
<h2>The formula</h2><p>EMI = P × r × (1 + r)<sup>n</sup> ÷ ((1 + r)<sup>n</sup> − 1), where P is the loan amount, r is the monthly interest rate (annual rate ÷ 12 ÷ 100) and n is the number of months.</p>
<h2>An illustrative example</h2><p>A loan of ₹5,00,000 at 12% p.a. for 3 years (36 months) has an EMI of about ₹16,607. Total repayment is about ₹5,97,860, so the total interest is about ₹97,860. This is illustrative only. Your actual EMI depends on the lender's terms.</p>
<h2>How interest and principal split</h2><p>In the early months most of the EMI is interest. Over time, more goes to principal. That is why prepaying early saves the most interest.</p>
<h2>Levers you control</h2><ul><li><strong>Tenure:</strong> a longer tenure lowers EMI but raises total interest.</li><li><strong>Rate:</strong> even 1% can change total cost significantly on a long loan.</li><li><strong>Part-prepayment:</strong> reduces principal and either shortens tenure or lowers EMI.</li></ul>
<p>Try different numbers with our <a href="/tools/emi-calculator/">EMI calculator</a>.</p>"""),
dict(slug="loan-documents-checklist-india", title="Loan Documents Checklist for India: Personal, Home, Business and More", cat="Documents",
 desc="Complete document checklist for each major loan type in India, including KYC, income, bank statements and property papers.",
 body="""<p>Complete, matching documents are the fastest way to speed up approval. Use this checklist, or our interactive <a href="/tools/document-checklist/">document checklist tool</a>.</p>
<h2>Common to every loan</h2><ul><li>PAN card and Aadhaar</li><li>Current address proof (Aadhaar, utility bill, rent agreement)</li><li>Recent passport-size photographs</li><li>Bank statements for the last 6 months</li></ul>
<h2>Salaried applicants</h2><ul><li>Last 3 to 6 salary slips</li><li>Form 16 and the last 2 years' ITR, if requested</li><li>Employment ID or offer letter</li></ul>
<h2>Self-employed and business owners</h2><ul><li>ITR with computation for 2 to 3 years</li><li>Balance sheet and profit and loss account</li><li>GST registration and returns, Udyam registration</li><li>Business address proof and ownership or rent agreement</li></ul>
<h2>Property-backed loans</h2><ul><li>Title and sale deeds, previous chain of documents</li><li>Approved building plan, property tax receipts</li><li>Encumbrance certificate</li><li>Society or builder NOC</li></ul>
<h2>Vehicle loans</h2><ul><li>Dealer quotation or proforma invoice</li><li>Driving licence (personal vehicles)</li><li>RC and insurance for used vehicles</li></ul>
<h2>Education loans</h2><ul><li>Admission letter, fee structure</li><li>Academic records and entrance scores</li><li>Co-applicant KYC and income proof</li></ul>
<p>Tip: make sure your name, date of birth and address match across all documents. Mismatches are a leading cause of delays.</p>"""),
dict(slug="secured-vs-unsecured-loans", title="Secured vs Unsecured Loans: Which Is Right for You?", cat="Choosing a loan",
 desc="Understand the difference between secured and unsecured loans, with examples, costs, risks and when to choose each.",
 body="""<p>A <strong>secured loan</strong> is backed by an asset such as a home, gold, a vehicle or securities. An <strong>unsecured loan</strong> relies only on your income and credit history.</p>
<h2>Quick comparison</h2><ul><li><strong>Interest rate:</strong> secured loans are usually cheaper.</li><li><strong>Amount:</strong> secured loans can be larger, because the asset supports them.</li><li><strong>Tenure:</strong> secured loans offer longer tenures.</li><li><strong>Speed:</strong> unsecured loans are often faster, with less verification.</li><li><strong>Risk:</strong> if you default on a secured loan, you can lose the asset.</li></ul>
<h2>Examples</h2><p>Secured: <a href="/loans/home-loan/">home loan</a>, <a href="/loans/loan-against-property/">loan against property</a>, <a href="/loans/gold-loan/">gold loan</a>, <a href="/loans/vehicle-loan/">vehicle loan</a>. Unsecured: <a href="/loans/personal-loan/">personal loan</a>, many small business loans and instant credit.</p>
<h2>Which should you choose?</h2><p>If you need a large amount for many years and own an asset, a secured loan is typically cheaper. If you need a smaller amount quickly, or do not want to pledge assets, an unsecured loan may be better. Whichever you choose, borrow only what your income can comfortably repay.</p>"""),
dict(slug="fixed-vs-floating-interest-rate", title="Fixed vs Floating Interest Rate: Which Is Better?", cat="Choosing a loan",
 desc="How fixed and floating rates work, how repo-linked rates reset, and how to choose between them.",
 body="""<p>A <strong>fixed rate</strong> stays the same for an agreed period. A <strong>floating rate</strong> moves with a benchmark, so your EMI or tenure can change when market rates change.</p>
<h2>How floating rates work in India</h2><p>For retail floating-rate loans, RBI requires lenders to link rates to an external benchmark, such as the repo rate. The lender adds a spread. When the benchmark falls, your rate resets after a defined period, and the reverse is also true.</p>
<h2>Pros and cons</h2><ul><li><strong>Fixed:</strong> predictable EMI but often a higher starting rate, and many 'fixed' loans reset after a few years.</li><li><strong>Floating:</strong> lower starting rate and benefits when rates fall, but EMIs can rise.</li></ul>
<h2>Which suits whom?</h2><p>Long loans such as home loans are mostly floating, with the advantage that prepayment on floating-rate loans to individuals does not attract prepayment charges under RBI rules. Short loans, such as personal loans, are usually fixed. Read the reset terms in the sanction letter.</p>"""),
dict(slug="apr-vs-interest-rate-key-fact-statement", title="APR vs Interest Rate and the Key Fact Statement, Explained", cat="Transparency",
 desc="Learn what APR is, why the interest rate alone is misleading, and how to read the RBI-mandated Key Fact Statement before taking a loan.",
 body="""<p>A loan's headline interest rate rarely tells the full story. Processing fees, insurance, documentation charges and other costs raise what you actually pay.</p>
<h2>What is APR?</h2><p>The Annual Percentage Rate (APR) expresses the total yearly cost of the loan, including interest and charges, as a single percentage. Comparing APRs is more reliable than comparing interest rates.</p>
<h2>The Key Fact Statement (KFS)</h2><p>Under RBI's digital lending rules, lenders must give borrowers a standardised Key Fact Statement before the contract is executed. It shows the loan amount, tenure, EMI, APR, all fees and charges, the repayment schedule, the cooling-off period and the grievance contact. Read it fully.</p>
<h2>What to check</h2><ul><li>APR, not just interest rate</li><li>Amount actually disbursed after deductions</li><li>Prepayment and foreclosure terms</li><li>Penal charges for late payment</li><li>Name of the regulated lender and any lending service provider</li></ul>
<p>At NEXUS, we will always point you to the KFS and encourage you to compare it across lenders.</p>"""),
dict(slug="how-to-avoid-loan-fraud-and-fake-apps", title="How to Avoid Loan Fraud, Fake Loan Apps and Advance-Fee Scams", cat="Safety",
 desc="Red flags of fake loan offers and apps, how to verify a lender in India, and what to do if you have been cheated.",
 body="""<p>Loan fraud is common. Scammers promise guaranteed approval, ask for an upfront fee, or harvest your phone data through unregulated apps. Stay safe with these rules.</p>
<h2>Red flags</h2><ul><li>'Guaranteed approval' regardless of credit score</li><li>Any request for an advance fee, 'insurance', 'GST' or 'processing' before disbursal</li><li>Requests for your OTP, PIN or card details</li><li>Apps that ask for access to your contacts, gallery or messages</li><li>Pressure to decide immediately</li><li>Unknown lender names and no clear grievance contact</li></ul>
<h2>How to verify</h2><ul><li>Check that the lender is a bank or an NBFC registered with RBI, and that the app appears in RBI's list of digital lending apps where applicable.</li><li>Look for the lender's name in the Key Fact Statement.</li><li>Loan disbursal must go directly into your bank account, and repayments directly to the lender, not to a third party or a personal account.</li></ul>
<h2>What NEXUS will never do</h2><ul><li>Ask you for an advance fee to approve a loan</li><li>Ask for your OTP, PIN, CVV or passwords</li><li>Promise guaranteed approval or the lowest rate</li><li>Receive loan money on your behalf</li></ul>
<h2>If you have been cheated</h2><p>Report immediately at the national cybercrime portal (cybercrime.gov.in) or call 1930. You can also report unauthorised lending apps on RBI's Sachet portal (sachet.rbi.org.in), and inform your bank at once if money was transferred.</p>"""),
dict(slug="rbi-digital-lending-guidelines-explained", title="RBI Digital Lending Guidelines Explained in Simple Words", cat="Regulation",
 desc="What RBI's digital lending rules mean for borrowers: Key Fact Statement, direct disbursal, data limits, cooling-off period and grievance redressal.",
 body="""<p>RBI has set rules for digital lending to protect borrowers. The details are in RBI's digital lending directions, which are updated from time to time, so treat this as a plain-language summary and not legal advice.</p>
<h2>Key protections for borrowers</h2><ul><li><strong>Regulated lender:</strong> only banks and NBFCs registered with RBI can lend. Platforms like NEXUS act only as facilitators.</li><li><strong>Disclosure:</strong> the lender must disclose the identity of lending service providers and give you a Key Fact Statement with APR and all costs.</li><li><strong>Direct flows:</strong> loan disbursal and repayment should flow directly between your bank account and the lender, not through a third party.</li><li><strong>Fees:</strong> fees payable to a lending service provider should be paid by the lender, not charged to you separately.</li><li><strong>Cooling-off period:</strong> a window during which you can exit the loan by repaying the principal and proportionate interest without penalty. Its length depends on loan tenure.</li><li><strong>Data:</strong> lenders and their partners can collect only the data needed, with your explicit consent, and must allow you to withdraw consent.</li><li><strong>Grievances:</strong> the lender must have a grievance officer. If not resolved in time, you can approach RBI's complaint management system.</li></ul>
<h2>What it means for you</h2><p>Ask for the KFS, check the lender's name, and never pay anyone other than the lender. Use our <a href="/grievance-redressal/">grievance page</a> if you have a concern about our service.</p>"""),
dict(slug="loan-foreclosure-and-part-prepayment", title="Loan Foreclosure and Part-Prepayment: Rules, Charges and Savings", cat="Repayment",
 desc="How to close a loan early or prepay part of it, what charges may apply, and when it makes sense.",
 body="""<p><strong>Foreclosure</strong> means closing the whole loan early. <strong>Part-prepayment</strong> means paying a lump sum towards principal to cut interest, tenure or EMI.</p>
<h2>Do lenders charge for it?</h2><p>For floating-rate loans to individuals, RBI rules bar prepayment charges, and RBI has extended similar protection to certain loans to micro and small enterprises, subject to conditions. Fixed-rate loans and some business loans may carry foreclosure charges, often 2% to 5% of the outstanding principal. Check your sanction letter and the KFS.</p>
<h2>When prepaying makes sense</h2><ul><li>Your loan interest rate is higher than what savings earn</li><li>You have received a bonus or windfall</li><li>The prepayment charge is low or nil</li></ul>
<h2>When to be careful</h2><ul><li>Do not drain your emergency fund</li><li>Compare against tax benefits on home or education loan interest</li><li>Check if the lender reduces EMI or tenure after prepayment, and choose what suits you</li></ul>
<p>Use our <a href="/tools/prepayment-calculator/">prepayment calculator</a> to see how much you could save.</p>"""),
dict(slug="personal-loan-vs-credit-card-vs-gold-loan", title="Personal Loan vs Credit Card EMI vs Gold Loan: Which Is Cheaper?", cat="Choosing a loan",
 desc="Compare personal loans, credit-card EMIs and gold loans on cost, speed, risk and documentation to choose the right option.",
 body="""<p>Three popular ways to raise money quickly are personal loans, credit-card EMIs and gold loans. Each has its own strengths.</p>
<h2>Personal loan</h2><p>Best for medium to large amounts with a defined repayment plan. Rates depend on your credit profile. No collateral needed.</p>
<h2>Credit card EMI</h2><p>Convenient for single purchases, but revolving balances carry among the highest interest rates in retail credit. EMI conversions usually cost less than revolving but still charge fees.</p>
<h2>Gold loan</h2><p>Fast and low on paperwork, with rates depending on the lender and the loan size. You must pledge gold, so there is a risk of losing it if you default.</p>
<h2>Rule of thumb</h2><ul><li>Planned, larger expense with good credit: personal loan.</li><li>Short-term gap and you own gold: gold loan, with a clear repayment date.</li><li>One-off purchase within 3 to 6 months: card EMI, only if the conversion fee is low.</li></ul>
<p>Compare real APRs using our <a href="/tools/compare-loans/">loan comparison tool</a>.</p>"""),
dict(slug="how-much-home-loan-can-i-get", title="How Much Home Loan Can I Get? Eligibility Explained", cat="Home loans",
 desc="Understand home loan eligibility: income multiples, FOIR, loan-to-value, age, credit score and ways to increase your eligible amount.",
 body="""<p>Lenders decide your home loan amount using three main checks: your repayment capacity, the property's value and your credit profile.</p>
<h2>1. Repayment capacity (FOIR)</h2><p>Fixed Obligations to Income Ratio (FOIR) compares your total monthly EMIs with your net monthly income. Many lenders allow roughly 40% to 55%, depending on income. After subtracting existing EMIs, the remaining room decides the new EMI and, in turn, the loan size for a given rate and tenure.</p>
<h2>2. Loan-to-value (LTV)</h2><p>RBI sets maximum loan-to-value ratios by loan size. A common band is up to 90% for smaller loans and 75% for larger ones. You pay the rest as down payment.</p>
<h2>3. Credit score, age and stability</h2><p>A score of 700+ is usually needed, and 750+ helps pricing. Age at maturity and job or business stability also matter.</p>
<h2>Ways to raise eligibility</h2><ul><li>Add a co-applicant with income</li><li>Close or reduce small loans and card balances</li><li>Choose a longer tenure, if the extra interest is acceptable</li><li>Include documented rental or other income</li></ul>
<p>Try our <a href="/tools/loan-eligibility-calculator/">eligibility calculator</a> for an indicative figure.</p>"""),
dict(slug="why-loan-applications-get-rejected", title="Why Loan Applications Get Rejected and How to Fix Them", cat="Credit score",
 desc="The most common reasons loans are rejected in India, such as low credit score, high FOIR, document mismatch, and what to do about each.",
 body="""<p>A rejection is not the end. It is information. Here are the common reasons and the fixes.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Low credit score or defaults:</strong> repay overdue amounts, wait for reporting to update and apply later.</li><li><strong>High existing EMIs (high FOIR):</strong> close small loans first or add a co-applicant.</li><li><strong>Unstable income or short job history:</strong> wait until you complete the lender's minimum tenure.</li><li><strong>Document mismatch:</strong> align name, date of birth and address across PAN, Aadhaar and bank records.</li><li><strong>Too many recent enquiries:</strong> pause for 3 to 6 months.</li><li><strong>Property or collateral issues:</strong> resolve title defects or unapproved construction.</li><li><strong>Employer or business category:</strong> some lenders have negative lists, so try a lender with a wider appetite.</li></ul>
<h2>What to do next</h2><p>Ask the lender for the reason, check your credit report and correct what you can. Avoid applying everywhere at once. <a href="/apply/">Share your profile with us</a> and we will suggest lenders whose criteria match.</p>"""),
dict(slug="how-to-choose-a-loan-agent-or-dsa", title="What Is a DSA? How to Choose a Trustworthy Loan Agent in India", cat="Safety",
 desc="What a Direct Selling Agent does, how DSAs are paid, red flags to watch for and questions to ask before sharing your documents.",
 body="""<p>A Direct Selling Agent (DSA) is an intermediary who sources loan customers for banks and NBFCs and helps with applications and documentation. Good DSAs save you time. Bad ones cost you money and privacy.</p>
<h2>How DSAs are paid</h2><p>Typically the lender pays the DSA a commission when a loan is disbursed. Under RBI norms, borrowers should not be charged separately for the lender's lending service provider.</p>
<h2>Questions to ask any DSA</h2><ul><li>Which lenders are you authorised to work with? Can you show the authorisation?</li><li>Will I receive the lender's Key Fact Statement before signing?</li><li>Do you charge me anything? (A trustworthy answer is: no advance fees.)</li><li>How will you use and store my documents and data?</li><li>Who is the grievance officer?</li></ul>
<h2>Red flags</h2><ul><li>Asks for money in advance</li><li>Refuses to name the lender</li><li>Suggests misrepresenting income or documents</li><li>Asks you to transfer money to a personal account</li></ul>
<p>NEXUS is a loan facilitation service. We do not lend, we do not charge advance fees and we do not ask you to misstate anything.</p>"""),
dict(slug="msme-loan-eligibility-and-documents", title="MSME Loan Eligibility and Documents: Udyam, GST, ITR Explained", cat="Business loans",
 desc="Who qualifies as an MSME, what lenders check, and which documents like Udyam, GST and ITR you need for a business loan.",
 body="""<p>Micro, small and medium enterprises are classified by investment and turnover. The thresholds are set by the government and can be revised, so check the latest MSME definitions.</p>
<h2>Why Udyam registration matters</h2><p>Udyam registration is free, online and gives your business a government-recognised identity. It unlocks priority-sector lending, credit-guarantee-backed loans and several schemes.</p>
<h2>What lenders check</h2><ul><li>Business vintage (usually 1 to 3 years)</li><li>Turnover and profit trends, from ITR and GST returns</li><li>Bank statement conduct: inflows, bounced cheques, overdrafts</li><li>Owner's credit score and business credit history</li><li>Security or guarantee, where needed</li></ul>
<h2>Documents</h2><p>See our <a href="/loans/business-loan/">business loan page</a> and <a href="/tools/document-checklist/">checklist tool</a>.</p>
<h2>Tips</h2><ul><li>Keep personal and business accounts separate</li><li>File GST and ITR on time and keep them consistent with bank credits</li><li>Ask about credit-guarantee-backed collateral-free options</li></ul>"""),
dict(slug="moratorium-and-grace-period-explained", title="Moratorium, Grace Period and Cooling-Off Period: What They Mean", cat="Repayment",
 desc="Three terms that sound alike but work differently: loan moratorium, grace period and cooling-off period.",
 body="""<p>These three terms are often confused, but they work differently.</p>
<h2>Moratorium</h2><p>A planned period when you do not pay full EMIs, common in education loans (course period plus about 6 to 12 months) and some project or construction loans. Interest usually keeps accruing, and unpaid interest may be added to the principal.</p>
<h2>Grace period</h2><p>A short window after a due date, offered by some lenders or card issuers, before late fees or negative reporting. Not all loans have one.</p>
<h2>Cooling-off period</h2><p>Under RBI's digital lending rules, a period after disbursal when you can exit the loan by repaying the principal and proportionate interest, without penalty. The length depends on tenure and the lender's policy.</p>
<h2>Tip</h2><p>Always find these in your sanction letter and KFS. If you are in an education loan moratorium, paying simple interest during the course can reduce the final burden.</p>"""),
dict(slug="tax-benefits-on-loans-in-india", title="Tax Benefits on Loans in India: Home, Education and Business", cat="Tax",
 desc="A general overview of tax deductions linked to home loans, education loans and business loans in India, with a reminder to check current law.",
 body="""<p>Tax rules change with each budget and depend on the tax regime you choose. Use this as a general overview and confirm with a chartered accountant.</p>
<h2>Home loan</h2><p>Under the old tax regime, interest on a home loan for a self-occupied property and principal repayment may be deductible within statutory limits under specific sections. Such deductions are generally not available under the new regime, except for certain let-out property cases.</p>
<h2>Education loan</h2><p>Interest paid on an education loan for higher studies may be deductible for up to a specified number of years under the relevant section, subject to the law in force.</p>
<h2>Business loan</h2><p>Interest on a loan taken for business is usually allowed as a business expense, subject to the Income Tax Act and proper accounting.</p>
<h2>Personal loan</h2><p>Typically no deduction, unless the funds are used for a deductible purpose such as business or house purchase, and the documentation supports it.</p>
<p><em>This is general information, not tax advice.</em></p>"""),
dict(slug="what-is-a-good-credit-score-in-india", title="What Is a Good Credit Score in India? Score Ranges Explained", cat="Credit score",
 desc="A plain-language guide to credit score ranges, what lenders consider good, and how your score affects loan approval and interest rates.",
 body="""<p>Credit scores in India typically range from 300 to 900. Each bureau has its own model, so your score may differ slightly between bureaus.</p>
<h2>Common bands</h2><ul><li><strong>750 and above:</strong> considered strong. Wider approval and the best pricing.</li><li><strong>700 to 749:</strong> good. Most mainstream lenders will consider you.</li><li><strong>650 to 699:</strong> fair. Approval is possible, often at higher rates or with conditions.</li><li><strong>Below 650:</strong> weak. Approval is harder, and secured loans or a co-applicant can help.</li><li><strong>NH/NA:</strong> no history. You are new to credit, and starting small helps.</li></ul>
<p>Lenders set their own cut-offs, so these bands are indicative, not rules.</p>
<h2>How the score affects you</h2><p>A higher score can mean a better chance of approval, a lower interest rate and a higher loan amount. Even a half-point difference in rate can save a lot on a long-tenure loan.</p>
<p>See our guide to <a href="/guides/how-to-improve-cibil-score/">improving your credit score</a>.</p>"""),
]
